<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice of 11 November 1918</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as armistice · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armistice of 11 November 1918 was the agreement that ended fighting on the Western Front between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It was signed after Germany's military position collapsed and revolution broke out at home. It coincided with the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918.jpg" alt="Armistice of 11 November 1918" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armisticetrain_(slight_crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The position of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> had worsened rapidly since the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a> in August 1918. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>'s resistance collapsed, an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from the south became possible, and the Allied blockade caused food shortages and disorder at home. On 29 September the Army Command told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a> the situation was hopeless, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> demanded a ceasefire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Prince Maximilian of Baden</a> became chancellor on 3 October, and by 5 October Germany had asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> to negotiate on the basis of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a>. The sailors' revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> on 29 to 30 October spread across Germany, and on 9 November Prince Max announced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>'s abdication and handed power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The German delegation, led by the civilian politician Matthias Erzberger, reached Ferdinand Foch's private train in the Forest of Compiègne on 8 November. The Germans were given 72 hours to agree, and there was little negotiation. Ebert told Erzberger to sign on 10 November. The armistice was agreed at 5:00 a.m. on 11 November and took effect at 11:00 a.m. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> time.</p><p>Its 34 clauses ended hostilities on the Western Front and required German evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Luxembourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> within 15 days, and of the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, with bridgeheads at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Germany had to surrender 5,000 artillery pieces, 1,700 aircraft and all its submarines, and the naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> continued. It was extended three times before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> took effect on 10 January 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Fighting went on until 11 a.m., and 2,738 men died on the last day of the war. Reactions at the front were muted, with silence and emptiness after 52 months. The German High Command laid the blame on others, including the socialists in government, which unsettled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> and fed the stab-in-the-back myth. 11 November is commemorated as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-day">Armistice Day</a>, Remembrance Day or Veterans Day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armistice of 11 November 1918” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In late October 1918 the empire dissolved from within: Czechoslovakia declared independence, the State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs was proclaimed, and Hungary ended the union, while the Italian victory at Vittorio Veneto led to the <b>armistice</b> of Villa Giusti on 3 November.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Paul Reynaud replaced Daladier in March 1940, and after the German breakthrough of May and June he refused to support an <b>armistice</b> and resigned on 16 June.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…and short of supplies after forces from the Belgian Congo had attacked from the west, in the Battle of Tabora, Lettow-Vorbeck abandoned the colony, withdrew south into Portuguese Mozambique and then into Northern Rhodesia, and agreed to a ceasefire on hearing of the <b>armistice</b> concluded three days earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Wilhelm II's abdication was announced on 9 November, Philipp Scheidemann proclaimed the German Republic the same day, and the <b>armistice</b> followed two days later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>It declared war on Germany, concluded an <b>armistice</b> with the Allies, set up People's Tribunals and carried out a land reform in March 1945, under the Allied Control Commission led by Marshal Kliment Voroshilov.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>armistice</b> with the Allies, signed at Cassibile on 3 September and announced on 8 September 1943, was followed by the flight of the king and government from Rome to Brindisi, leaving the army without orders as Germany occupied central and northern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
